To make the spell values (like damage, level, or heal amount) **changeable from elsewhere in the program**, you can modify your `Spell` class to allow for **mutability** (i.e., the ability to change the values after creation). Currently, your `Spell` class likely has **getter methods** but no **setter methods**, which means the values are **immutable**.

---

### ✅ Here's how to fix it:

#### 1. **Update the `Spell` class to include setter methods**

Assuming your `Spell` class looks something like this:

```java
public class Spell {
    private String name;
    private int damage;
    private int level;
    private boolean have;
    private int heal;

    public Spell(String name, int damage, int level, boolean have, int heal) {
        this.name = name;
        this.damage = damage;
        this.level = level;
        this.have = have;
        this.heal = heal;
    }

    // Getters
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public int getDamage() { return damage; }
    public int getLevel() { return level; }
    public boolean getHave() { return have; }
    public int getHeal() { return heal; }

    // Setters
    public void setDamage(int damage) { this.damage = damage; }
    public void setLevel(int level) { this.level = level; }
    public void setHave(boolean have) { this.have = have; }
    public void setHeal(int heal) { this.heal = heal; }
}
```

